> come
>
> so I take a sample of this big data set and sort it and end up with
> something like 6,9,12,12,14,15,18.

I don't think that sampling absolute times is the right approach in
general. Because what you get from that is not the original arrival
process, but a kind of decimation of the original.

> I then add on to this the first hour of
> the day, so for mondays this is 0, tuesdays this is 24, wednesdays is 48 etc
> etc.

You don't need to do that, because you are going to take differences.
Just add a zero at the beginning, always.

> Then my monday_data is calculated as diff(0,6,9,12,12,14,15,18) to give me
> an interarrival list time of 6,3,3,3,1,1,3.

I suppose you meant 6, 3, 3, 0, 2, 1, 3. As I said, it is ok to return
a zero, you don't need to add 0.01 or something like that.

> When I am passing the sample data straight in as a singular group of
> numbers, from what I can see it is taking the interarrival list and using
> the lowest interarrivals first and progressing to higher interarrival times.
> Is this how it works? maybe I am confused??
> So the customer ends up arriving at 1am(1), 2am (1), 5am(3) ,8am(3) etc etc,
> instead of using the sorted list order.

No, it's not. from_to takes the interarrivals and uses them in the
given order. Try it out yourself:

gen <- from_to(0, 24, function() c(6, 3, 3, 0, 2, 1, 3), arrive=FALSE,
every=168)
gen() # first week
#> [1] 6 3 3 0 2 1 3
gen() # second week
#> [1] 156   3   3   0   2   1   3

etc. As you can see, from_to returns exactly the interarrivals given.
It's just adding the appropriate lag between weeks.

> This was the reason originally I started passing through singular sample
> interarrival numbers. But what is happening is lets say I want 7 customers,
> I hit the 7th, execute the interarrival and returning null faults the
> generator, returning -1 seems to ruin things, returning 0 causes another
> arrival..! I have tried sending through a massive interarrival of 100,000,
> but this still causes my function to continue counting one more time on the
> wrong day, and then my counter goes out of wack :S. See 'fakie' below in the
> function
>
> distribution_fit <-

Again, you don't need this function at all. Just sample your data and
return the diffs. That's all. The rest is handled by from_to.

> I tried cbinding the day of week function call, and if this was not the same
> as the last cbind in the list then reset count and reproduce the new days
> answer, I dont think I executed it properly though but will give it another
> shot.
>
> What do you suggest I do in order to overcome this situation?? The situation
> being what do I return to make the daily generator go to the next generator
> once it is out of my requested daily customer arrivals?

Don't think about it in that way. You don't need to "go from one
generator to the other". All the generators are active all time. They
just spit arrivals at the right time thanks to from_to.

